 "batch_notes": "Numeric-library batch: dominated by algebraic laws (num-complex, num-rational), roundtrips (palette, num-bigint) and primitive-type oracles (BigInt vs i128, NotNan vs f64). Rubric gaps: (1) 'wrapper agrees with the primitive it wraps' (ordered-float, num-bigint vs i128) is filed as external-oracle since the stdlib primitive is outside the crate, but it sits between external-oracle and internal-consistency; (2) arithmetic inverse pairs (exp/ln, (a*b)/b, from_polar/to_polar) blur roundtrip vs algebraic-law; I used roundtrip only for representation conversions. (3) 'A never scores below B' (nucleo optimal vs greedy) is an inequality between two paths, neither agreement nor a law. num-complex is notable for deriving every tolerance from an explicit IEEE error budget in the doc comment. num-format properties have no doc comments at all (only leading body comments). num-rational relies on helpers (check_*) whose bodies are not in the batch, so four classifications there are from names. nucleo's pins are example tests but every one is cited by a property that gates around it, so all eight attributions are explicit; numfmt's pins are cited by properties for only 3 of 7 bugs. openraft/1's record names a removed hegel property (hegel_std_vote_partial_cmp_none_voted_for_panics) as its test, not an example pin."
}